From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: meanaverage <18336095+meanaverage@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 13:26:34 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 35/51] fix(graphics): cap the memory the Progressive decoder
 keeps at 1 GiB

The RemoteFX Progressive decoder keeps the state a server asks for: a
codec context, with a tile grid sized for its surface, for every
(surface, codec context id) the server names; a 36 KiB TileState for
every tile it fills; and a 24 KiB sub-band reference for every tile
position of a surface. Nothing bounded any of it, and only
DeleteEncodingContext, DeleteSurface or a reset freed it. A server could
open codec contexts on a 32768x32768 surface with empty payloads, 2 MiB
of tile grid each (1 MiB in the WebAssembly build), or fill tiles at
about 60 KiB per tile block of a few dozen bytes, until the WebAssembly
instance every session shares ran out of memory: on 64-bit, 600 such
codec contexts held 1.26 GB.

The decoder now counts what it holds (codec contexts, tile grids, tiles
and references) and refuses a codec context, grid, tile or reference
that would take it past MAX_PROGRESSIVE_BYTES, 1 GiB, with
ProgressiveDecodeError::MemoryLimitExceeded, which ends the session the
way a malformed tile does. Deleting a codec context or a surface,
resetting, and resizing a surface give the memory back. 1 GiB holds
every tile of the 256 MiB of 32 bpp surfaces the EGFX compositor
displays at most (64 Mi pixels, two 8K monitors), at about 60 KiB a
tile.

A codec context is charged the size of its map entry and of its grid's
slots, and CONTEXT_OVERHEAD_BYTES, 512, for what holding them takes
besides: B-tree nodes about half full, for the context and, while an
RDPGFX frame is open, its frame_tiles entry, and the grid's smallest
allocation. An empty codec context on a one-tile surface takes about 150
bytes (110 in the WebAssembly build) for a size of 48 (32): charged its
size alone, codec contexts that a server opens with empty payloads
inside a frame would hold more than three times the limit before
reaching it, 3.1 GiB (3.4 GiB of WebAssembly memory). With the 512
bytes, the 1.9 million it takes to reach the limit hold 274 MiB (209 MiB
of WebAssembly memory). Windows and GNOME Remote Desktop open one or two
codec contexts per surface.

For the count to match what is allocated, references are now boxed: a
B-tree node holds eleven values, and with 24 KiB values, the empty slots
of half-full nodes took up to as much memory again (a 4096-tile surface
held 326 MB, now 252 MB). A TILE_UPGRADE for a tile without a first pass
no longer allocates an empty tile.
